OAI_PAPER({"slug": "A-boundary-field-gap-for-the-spin-one-Heisenberg-chain-September-24-2026", "title": "A boundary-field gap for the spin-one Heisenberg chain", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/A-boundary-field-gap-for-the-spin-one-Heisenberg-chain-September-24-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "268", "family_title": "The spin-one Haldane gap", "abstract_html": "We prove a uniform spectral gap for odd open spin-one antiferromagnetic Heisenberg chains with the same fixed magnetic field at both endpoints. The field \\(h=3/5\\) gives a gap greater than \\(\\log(10)/392\\) on every chain of \\(2L+1\\) sites with <i>L</i> ≥ 960. Tasaki's index theorem then gives index −1 for every subsequential local limit of these boundary-selected ground states.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( E_1(L,3/5)-E_0(L,3/5)> \\frac{\\log 10}{392} \\qquad\\text{for every integer }L\\ge960. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">In the orthonormal basis \\(e_m\\), \\(m=-1,0,1\\), of \\(\\mathbb C^3\\), let\n  \\[\n  S^ze_m=me_m,\\qquad\n  S^+e_m=\\sqrt{2-m(m+1)}\\,e_{m+1}\\quad(m&lt;1),\\qquad S^+e_1=0.\n  \\]\n  Put\n  \\[\n  S^-=(S^+)^*,\\qquad S^x=\\frac{S^++S^-}{2},\\qquad\n  S^y=\\frac{S^+-S^-}{2i}.\n  \\]\n  Thus \\(\\sum_{\\alpha=x,y,z}(S^\\alpha)^2=2I\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For an integer \\(L\\ge1\\) and \\(h&gt;0\\), define the open-chain Hamiltonian on\n  \\((\\mathbb C^3)^{\\otimes(2L+1)}\\) by\n  \\[\n  H_L(h)=\n  \\sum_{j=-L}^{L-1}\\sum_{\\alpha=x,y,z}S_j^\\alpha S_{j+1}^\\alpha\n  -h(S_{-L}^z+S_L^z).\n  \\]\n  There is no bond between the endpoints.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E_0(L,h)\\le E_1(L,h)\\) be the two lowest eigenvalues, counted with multiplicity.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nE_1(L,3/5)-E_0(L,3/5)&gt;\n\\frac{\\log 10}{392}\n\\qquad\\text{for every integer }L\\ge960.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix an odd integer \\(n\\in[M_j,2M_j]\\) with \\(j\\ge5\\). Let\n\\[\np_0\\ge p_1\\ge\\cdots\n\\]\nbe the Gibbs probabilities of \\(H_n^{\\mathrm{s}}\\) at inverse temperature \\(b_j\\), listing energies with multiplicity.</p><p>The left side of Proposition 8 is \\(\\sum_ip_i^2\\). Since\n\\[\n\\sum_ip_i^2\\le p_0\\sum_ip_i=p_0,\n\\]\none has\n\\[\np_0\\ge1-4x_j&gt;1/2.\n\\]\nIn particular, the physical ground state is simple. Thus, writing \\(E_0,E_1\\) for the two lowest energies of \\(H_n^{\\mathrm{s}}\\),\n\\[\ne^{-b_j(E_1-E_0)}\n=\\frac{p_1}{p_0}\n\\le\\frac{1-p_0}{p_0}\n\\le\\frac{4x_j}{1-4x_j}\n&lt;10^{-2^{j-5}}.\n\\]\nFor the strict final inequality, use\n\\[\nx_j=10^{-1-2^{j-5}},\n\\qquad \\frac4{10(1-.04)}&lt;1.\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\nb_j=392\\,2^{j-5},\n\\]\ntaking logarithms yields\n\\[\nE_1-E_0&gt;\\frac{\\log10}{392}.\n\\]</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\nM_5=1920,\\qquad M_{j+1}=2M_j.\n\\]\nThe intervals \\([M_j,2M_j]\\), \\(j\\ge5\\), cover every integer \\(n\\ge1920\\). Every odd length \\(n=2L+1\\) with \\(L\\ge960\\) is therefore covered. Relabeling the sites identifies \\(H_n^{\\mathrm{s}}\\) with \\(H_L(3/5)\\), with the same single field \\(h=3/5\\) at every length.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 7.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every boundary-selected state \\(\\omega\\) is invariant under uniform rotations about the \\(z\\) axis and under reflection \\(j\\mapsto-j\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the spin normalization and Hamiltonians \\(H_L(3/5)\\) of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(L\\ge960\\), let \\(\\phi_L\\) be a normalized ground vector and define\n  \\[\n  \\omega_L(A)=\\langle\\phi_L,A\\phi_L\\rangle\n  \\]\n  for observables supported in \\([-L,L]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A local observable is a matrix acting on finitely many sites of \\(\\mathbb Z\\), extended by the identity elsewhere.</p><p class=\"li\">A boundary-selected state is any subsequential local limit\n  \\[\n  \\omega(A)=\\lim_{k\\to\\infty}\\omega_{L_k}(A),\\qquad L_k\\to\\infty,\n  \\]\n  existing for every local observable \\(A\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  H_{\\mathrm{bulk}}=\\sum_{j\\in\\mathbb Z}S_j\\cdot S_{j+1}\n  \\]\n  be the formal bulk Hamiltonian. For local \\(A\\), the commutator\n  \\([H_{\\mathrm{bulk}},A]\\) is the finite sum over bonds meeting the support of \\(A\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  \\gamma_*=\\frac{\\log 10}{392}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), define the local twist\n  \\[\n  U_\\varepsilon=\n  \\exp\\left[\n  -i\\sum_{j\\in\\mathbb Z\\cap[-\\pi/\\varepsilon,\\pi/\\varepsilon]}\n  (\\pi+\\varepsilon j)S_j^z\n  \\right].\n  \\]\n  Rotations by \\(0\\) and \\(2\\pi\\) outside this interval act trivially for spin one. The twist convention is that of [20, Equations (10)–(13)], where [20] is Tasaki, “Ground state of the \\(S=1\\) antiferromagnetic Heisenberg chain is topologically nontrivial if gapped,” with numbering following arXiv:2407.17041v4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every boundary-selected state \\(\\omega\\) is invariant under uniform rotations about the \\(z\\) axis and under reflection \\(j\\mapsto-j\\). Moreover,\n\\[\n\\omega\\!\\left(A^*[H_{\\mathrm{bulk}},A]\\right)\n\\ge\n\\gamma_*\\left(\\omega(A^*A)-|\\omega(A)|^2\\right)\n\\qquad\\text{for every local }A.\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(\\omega\\) is a locally-unique gapped ground state in the sense of [20, Definition 11], and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ind}(\\omega)\n=\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}\\omega(U_\\varepsilon)\n=-1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Theorem 1 makes the finite ground vector unique up to phase for \\(L\\ge960\\), so \\(\\omega_L\\) is well defined. Compactness of density matrices on each finite interval, followed by a diagonal subsequence over the intervals, gives subsequences for which all local expectations converge. The limits are consistent on nested intervals and define a state. No convergence of the full sequence or uniqueness of the infinite-volume state is presumed.</p><p>Uniform rotations about \\(z\\) and reflection \\(j\\mapsto-j\\) commute with \\(H_L(3/5)\\). Simplicity of its ground eigenspace implies invariance of \\(\\omega_L\\) under these symmetries. Passing to the subsequential local limit proves the stated invariances.</p><p>For \\(L\\ge960\\), the finite spectral theorem and Theorem 1 give\n\\[\n\\langle A\\phi_L,\n(H_L(3/5)-E_0(L,3/5))A\\phi_L\\rangle\n\\ge\n\\gamma_*\n\\left(\\omega_L(A^*A)-|\\omega_L(A)|^2\\right).\n\\]\nThe left side equals\n\\[\n\\omega_L(A^*[H_L(3/5),A]).\n\\]\nOnce the support of \\(A\\) and its adjacent sites lie strictly inside \\([-L,L]\\), this commutator equals \\([H_{\\mathrm{bulk}},A]\\): the endpoint fields and all omitted bonds commute with \\(A\\). Taking the subsequential limit gives\n\\[\n\\omega(A^*[H_{\\mathrm{bulk}},A])\n\\ge\n\\gamma_*\\left(\\omega(A^*A)-|\\omega(A)|^2\\right).\n\\]\nThe right side is nonnegative by Cauchy–Schwarz for a state. For \\(\\omega(A)=0\\), this is the local gap inequality. The strict finite-volume bound has yielded the non-strict lower bound \\(\\gamma_*\\) in the limit.</p><p>The Hamiltonians, spin normalization, and endpoint-field signs are precisely those of [20, Equation (2)]. Theorem 1 verifies [20, Assumption 2] with\n\\[\nh=\\frac35,\\qquad L_0=960,\\qquad\\gamma=\\gamma_*.\n\\]\nTherefore [20, Theorem 3], with the subsequential interpretation in its End Matter, Equation (27), applies to every boundary-selected state.</p><p>For the sign and order of limits, Tasaki’s finite-chain twist interpolation begins at the uniform \\(\\pi\\) rotation. Its expectation is \\(-1\\), because the finite ground state has total \\(S^z=1\\) by [20, Lemma 1]. Reflection makes the twist expectation real, and the uniform gap prevents it from crossing zero while the twist parameter is sufficiently small.</p><p>More precisely, [20, Lemmas 6–8 and Equations (17)–(18)] gives, for every sufficiently small fixed \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\) and all sufficiently large \\(L\\),\n\\[\n-1\\le\\omega_L(U_\\varepsilon)\n\\le\n-\\sqrt{1-\\frac{4(\\pi+\\varepsilon)\\varepsilon}{\\gamma_*}}.\n\\]\nHere \\(L\\ge\\pi/\\varepsilon\\) ensures that the finite-chain twist equals the local operator in the theorem statement. Passing first to the chosen subsequential local limit and then letting \\(\\varepsilon\\downarrow0\\) gives\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ind}(\\omega)=-1.\n\\]</p><p>This conclusion concerns the boundary-selected states. It does not require the full sequence \\(\\omega_L\\) to converge, identify these states with every infinite-chain ground state, or assume the additional global uniqueness hypothesis of [20, Corollary 4].</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Proposition 2.1; transfer setting from Sections 2.1–2.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The operator \\(X_b\\) is real, self-adjoint, and Hilbert–Schmidt.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the spin-one matrices of Theorem 1 and set\n  \\[\n  h=\\frac35,\\qquad\n  a=\\frac{700741}{500000},\\qquad\n  b_0=\\frac{49}{4},\\qquad b_j=2^jb_0\\quad(j\\ge0).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Label an \\(n\\)-site chain by \\(0,\\ldots,n-1\\), and write\n  \\(S_j\\cdot S_k=\\sum_{\\alpha=x,y,z}S_j^\\alpha S_k^\\alpha\\). Define\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  H_n^{\\mathrm{opp}}\n  &amp;=\\sum_{r=0}^{n-2}S_r\\cdot S_{r+1}-hS_0^z+hS_{n-1}^z,\\\\\n  H_n^{\\mathrm{s}}\n  &amp;=\\sum_{r=0}^{n-2}S_r\\cdot S_{r+1}-hS_0^z-hS_{n-1}^z,\\\\\n  H_n^{\\mathrm{per}}\n  &amp;=\\sum_{r=0}^{n-2}S_r\\cdot S_{r+1}+S_{n-1}\\cdot S_0\n  \\qquad(n\\ge4).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n  For \\(n=2L+1\\), \\(H_n^{\\mathrm{s}}\\) is \\(H_L(3/5)\\) after relabeling.</p><p class=\"li\">The shifted partition functions are ordinary traces over the entire physical Hilbert space:\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  Z_n(b)&amp;=\\operatorname{Tr}e^{-b(H_n^{\\mathrm{per}}+anI)},\\\\\n  Y_n(b)&amp;=\\operatorname{Tr}e^{-b(H_n^{\\mathrm{opp}}+anI)},\\\\\n  Y_n^{\\mathrm{s}}(b)&amp;=\\operatorname{Tr}e^{-b(H_n^{\\mathrm{s}}+anI)}.\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">In the unitarily equivalent Cartesian basis, let\n  \\[\n  (S^\\alpha)_{rs}=-i\\varepsilon_{\\alpha rs},\\qquad G_\\alpha=iS^\\alpha.\n  \\]\n  Then \\(G_\\alpha\\) is real, \\(\\|G_\\alpha\\|=1\\), and\n  \\[\n  -S\\cdot S=\\sum_{\\alpha=x,y,z}G_\\alpha\\otimes G_\\alpha.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(b&gt;0\\), a history is an ordered list\n  \\[\n  \\omega=((t_1,\\alpha_1),\\ldots,(t_d,\\alpha_d)),\n  \\quad 0&lt;t_1&lt;\\cdots&lt;t_d&lt;b,\\quad \\alpha_r\\in\\{x,y,z\\},\n  \\]\n  including the empty list. Let \\(\\Omega_b\\) be the disjoint union of these labeled simplexes, with Lebesgue measure on each simplex and mass one at the empty history. Denote this measure by \\(\\mu_b\\); its total mass is \\(e^{3b}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For two histories, merge the events chronologically and take the trace of the corresponding product of \\(G\\) matrices, with increasing time from left to right. Denote the resulting real kernel by \\(k_b(\\omega,\\eta)\\). Set it to zero at nonempty time collisions, and set its value at two empty histories to \\(3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">On \\(\\mathcal K_b=L^2(\\Omega_b,\\mu_b;\\mathbb C)\\), define\n  \\[\n  (X_bf)(\\omega)\n  =e^{-ab}\\int_{\\Omega_b}k_b(\\omega,\\eta)f(\\eta)\\,d\\mu_b(\\eta).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D_2\\) consist of the identity and the three diagonal rotations by \\(\\pi\\), let \\(C\\) cyclically permute the coordinate axes, and put\n  \\(\\mathcal G=\\langle D_2,C\\rangle\\). This group has twelve elements.\n  If\n  \\[\n  ge_\\alpha=\\epsilon_g(\\alpha)e_{\\sigma_g(\\alpha)},\n  \\]\n  let \\(\\sigma_g\\omega\\) relabel the marks and set\n  \\[\n  s_g(\\omega)=\\prod_r\\epsilon_g(\\alpha_r),\\qquad\n  (U_gf)(\\omega)=s_g(\\sigma_g^{-1}\\omega)f(\\sigma_g^{-1}\\omega).\n  \\]\n  Put \\(P=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1)\\), so \\(Pe_z=-e_z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define the boundary vector\n  \\[\n  v_b(\\omega)=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  (ih)^d,&amp;\\text{all \\(d\\) marks of \\(\\omega\\) equal \\(z\\)},\\\\\n  0,&amp;\\text{otherwise}.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]\n  Its empty-history value is one. Inner products are conjugate linear in the first argument.</p><p class=\"li\">The periodic transfer identity imported from [16, Proposition 3.1] is\n  \\[\n  Z_n(b)=\\operatorname{Tr}X_b^n\\quad(n\\ge4),\\qquad\n  \\sum_i|\\mu_i(b)|^k&lt;\\infty\\quad(k\\ge2),\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\mu_i(b)\\) lists the nonzero eigenvalues with multiplicity. Here [16] is the companion preprint “The periodic spin-one Haldane gap,” September 24, 2026.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The operator \\(X_b\\) is real, self-adjoint, and Hilbert–Schmidt. The operators \\(U_g\\) form a real unitary representation commuting with \\(X_b\\), and\n\\[\n\\|v_b\\|^2=e^{bh^2}.\n\\]\nFor \\(b&gt;0\\), \\(n\\ge2\\), and \\(g\\in\\mathcal G\\), the pairing\n\\[\n\\langle v_b,X_b^nU_gv_b\\rangle\n\\]\nis the shifted partition function of the open chain with left field \\(-hS_0^z\\) and right field \\(+hS_{n-1}^{ge_z}\\), where\n\\(S^u=\\sum_\\alpha u_\\alpha S^\\alpha\\). Consequently,\n\\[\nY_n(b)=\\langle v_b,X_b^nv_b\\rangle,\\qquad\nY_n^{\\mathrm{s}}(b)=\\langle v_b,X_b^nU_Pv_b\\rangle.\n\\]\nFor even \\(n\\),\n\\[\nY_n(b)=\\langle v_b,|X_b|^nv_b\\rangle.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Exchanging the two histories leaves their merged list unchanged. Thus \\(k_b\\) is symmetric; symmetry does not reverse a product of noncommuting matrices. Moreover, \\(|k_b|\\le3\\). Since \\(\\mu_b(\\Omega_b)=e^{3b}&lt;\\infty\\), the operator is real, self-adjoint, and Hilbert–Schmidt.</p><p>Rotating every matrix in the kernel proves that the real unitaries \\(U_g\\) commute with \\(X_b\\); their definition gives the representation law. The simplex volumes give\n\\[\n\\|v_b\\|^2=\\sum_{d\\ge0}\\frac{b^dh^{2d}}{d!}=e^{bh^2}.\n\\]</p><p>Expand the negative Hamiltonian exponential into its ordered-time series. Each bond event inserts two \\(G_\\alpha\\) matrices, by the Cartesian identity above. At the left endpoint, the conjugated coefficient in the boundary vector gives\n\\[\n(-ih)G_z=+hS^z\n\\]\nper event; at the right endpoint the coefficient gives\n\\[\n(ih)G_z=-hS^z.\n\\]\nRelabeling by \\(g\\) and multiplying its signs changes this last insertion to \\(-hS^{ge_z}\\). These are exactly the two field insertions in the negative Hamiltonian stated in the proposition.</p><p>Regroup the series by the boundary histories \\(\\omega_0,\\omega_n\\) and the \\(n-1\\) bond histories \\(\\omega_1,\\ldots,\\omega_{n-1}\\). Their interleavings partition each full time simplex up to null collisions. At site \\(r\\), \\(0\\le r&lt;n\\), the trace is \\(k_b(\\omega_r,\\omega_{r+1})\\), in the original chronological order. Integrating these \\(n\\) kernel factors against the two boundary coefficients gives \\(X_b^n\\) and the shift factor \\(e^{-ban}\\). No bond joins the endpoints.</p><p>This regrouping is legitimate: the absolute sum of traced terms is bounded by\n\\[\n3^n\\exp\\{b[3(n-1)+2h]\\}\n\\]\nbefore the scalar shift. All sums and integrals are therefore absolutely convergent. Choosing \\(g=1\\) and \\(g=P\\) proves the two boundary identities.</p><p>For even \\(n\\), self-adjointness gives \\(X_b^n=|X_b|^n\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 3.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(j\\ge0\\), \\(X_{b_j}\\) has a unique simple eigenvalue of largest modulus.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(X_b\\), \\(Z_n(b)\\), \\(\\mathcal G\\), and\n  \\[\n  b_j=2^j\\frac{49}{4}\n  \\]\n  from Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">For even \\(n\\ge4\\), define\n  \\[\n  S(n,b)=\\frac{Z_{2n}(b)}{Z_n(b)^2},\\qquad\n  T(n,b)=\\frac{Z_n(2b)}{Z_n(b)^2}.\n  \\]\n  These are, respectively, the purities of the spatial probabilities\n  \\(|\\mu_i(b)|^n/Z_n(b)\\) and the physical Gibbs probabilities.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  f(u)=\\frac{u^2}{2(1-u)^2}\\quad(0\\le u&lt;1),\\qquad\n  d(u)=\\frac{u}{2-3u}\\quad(0\\le u\\le.1).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  \\ell=.999,\\quad U=1.00139,\\quad V=.872,\\quad\n  m_J=1.0657205,\\quad m_N=.2783155.\n  \\]\n  Every terminating decimal in this outline denotes an exact rational number.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(j\\ge2\\), put\n  \\[\n  n_j=120\\,2^{j-2},\\qquad M_j=2n_j,\\qquad\n  u_2=.052,\\qquad u_{j+1}=6u_j^2.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(j\\ge0\\), \\(X_{b_j}\\) has a unique simple eigenvalue of largest modulus. It is positive; write\n\\[\n\\lambda_j=\\|X_{b_j}\\|.\n\\]\nIts eigenspace is fixed pointwise by \\(\\mathcal G\\). If \\(\\rho_j\\) is the supremum of all other absolute eigenvalues divided by \\(\\lambda_j\\), then\n\\[\n\\ell&lt;\\lambda_0\\le U,\\qquad\n\\rho_0&lt;.874,\\qquad \\rho_1&lt;.9525,\\qquad \\rho_2&lt;.971.\n\\]\nFor every \\(j\\ge2\\),\n\\[\n1-S(n_j,b_j)\\le u_j,\\qquad\n1-T(M_j,b_j)\\le u_j,\\qquad\n\\rho_j^{n_j}\\le d(u_j).\n\\]\nFinally, if\n\\[\nr_j=\\frac{\\lambda_{j+1}}{\\lambda_j^2},\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n0&lt;r_j\\le1\\quad(j\\ge0),\\qquad\nr_j^{M_j}\\ge(1-u_j)(1-u_{j+1})\\quad(j\\ge2).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Until positivity has been proved, write \\(\\lambda_j=\\|X_{b_j}\\|\\) for the maximal spectral modulus.</p><p>First consider \\(b_0\\). Remove an eigenvalue in \\(J\\) whose modulus exceeds \\(\\ell\\), as supplied by Lemma 2.3. The remaining twelfth-power mass in \\(J\\) is at most\n\\[\nm_J-\\ell^{12}&lt;\\ell^{12},\n\\]\nwhile \\(V&lt;\\ell\\) bounds each of the other three sectors. The removed eigenvalue is therefore the unique simple eigenvalue of maximal modulus. The exact rational inequalities\n\\[\nm_J-\\ell^{12}&lt;(.874\\ell)^{12},\\qquad V&lt;.874\\ell\n\\]\nprove \\(\\rho_0&lt;.874\\).</p><p>For every real \\(k\\ge12\\), define\n\\[\nD(k)=\n\\frac{(m_J-\\ell^{12})^{k/12}\n+3\\left[V^k+(m_N-V^{12})^{k/12}\\right]}{\\ell^k}.\n\\]\nThe sum of the \\(k\\)th powers of all nondominant absolute eigenvalue ratios at \\(b_0\\) is at most \\(D(k)\\). Indeed,\n\\[\nV^{12}&lt;m_N&lt;2V^{12}.\n\\]\nFor a nonnegative list with entries at most \\(V^{12}\\) and total mass at most \\(m_N\\), convexity of \\(x^{k/12}\\) bounds the sum by\n\\[\n(V^{12})^{k/12}+(m_N-V^{12})^{k/12}.\n\\]\nThis follows by moving mass to fill one entry before the next; finite sublists followed by a limit give the countable case. Without an entry cap, the corresponding bound is the total mass to the power \\(k/12\\). Applying these facts to the four sectors proves the claim. Thus, for even \\(k\\ge12\\),\n\\[\nZ_k(b_0)\\le U^k(1+D(k)).\n\\]</p><p>Next reseed at \\((120,b_2)\\). By Lemma 2.3 and Lemma 2.1, the spatial list at \\((72,b_1)\\) has a unique largest weight. Thus \\(X_{b_1}\\) has a unique simple eigenvalue of maximal modulus. Since\n\\[\n.0604996&lt;2(.032)(.968),\n\\]\nthat weight exceeds \\(.968\\). The periodic trial at length \\(72\\) gives \\(Z_{72}(b_1)&gt;1\\), so\n\\[\n\\lambda_1^{72}&gt;.968.\n\\]</p><p>Physical squaring at length \\(32\\) gives \\(Z_{32}(b_1)\\le Z_{32}(b_0)^2\\). Subtracting the dominant spatial contribution yields\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\ne0}\\left|\\frac{\\mu_i(b_1)}{\\lambda_1}\\right|^{32}\n\\le U^{64}(1+D(32))^2(.968)^{-32/72}-1\n&lt;.9525^{32}.\n\\]\nThis proves \\(\\rho_1&lt;.9525\\). For a nonnegative list \\((a_i)\\) and \\(q\\ge p&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_i a_i^q\\le\\left(\\sum_i a_i^p\\right)^{q/p}\n\\]\nwhenever the right side is finite. Applying this gives a residual sixtieth moment below \\(.9525^{60}\\). Retaining only the dominant term in the purity numerator gives\n\\[\nS(60,b_1)\\ge s:=(1+.9525^{60})^{-2}.\n\\]</p><p>For the physical spectrum at length \\(120\\), choose one ground Boltzmann weight at \\(b_0\\) and divide every other weight by it. The chosen weight is greater than one by Lemma 2.3. Hence the sum of the other ratios is at most\n\\[\nR=U^{120}(1+D(120))-1.\n\\]\nWhen \\(b\\) doubles, these ratios square, so their sum is at most \\(R^2\\). Keeping the chosen ground weight in the purity numerator gives\n\\[\nT(120,b_1)\\ge t:=(1+R^2)^{-2}.\n\\]\nThis neither assumes ground-state simplicity nor discards multiplicities.</p><p>The following rational checks suffice:\n\\[\n\\begin{gathered}\nD(32)&lt;.0442,\\qquad D(120)&lt;.00000025,\\qquad\n(.9856)^9&lt;(.968)^4,\\\\\nU^{64}(1+.0442)^2/.9856-1&lt;.2092&lt;.9525^{32},\\\\\ns&gt;.9002,\\qquad t&gt;.9372,\\\\\nf(1-.9002^2\\,.9372)&lt;.0502,\\\\\nf\\!\\left(1-.9372^2(1-.0502)\\right)&lt;.0198.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\]\nThe cube roots in \\(D(32)\\) are bounded by\n\\[\n(m_J-\\ell^{12})^{1/3}&lt;.426635,\\qquad\n(m_N-V^{12})^{1/3}&lt;.439736.\n\\]\nCubing verifies these two bounds; raising them to the eighth power gives the bound on \\(D(32)\\). For \\(D(120)\\), no roots are needed. The bound on \\(t\\) follows with \\(D(120)\\) replaced by \\(.00000025\\). Lemma 2.2, applied at \\((n,b)=(60,b_1)\\), therefore gives\n\\[\n1-S(120,b_2)&lt;.052,\\qquad 1-T(240,b_2)&lt;.052.\n\\]</p><p>For the induction, when \\(0\\le u\\le.052\\),\n\\[\nf\\!\\left(1-(1-u)^3\\right)=G(u)u^2,\\qquad\nG(u)=\\frac12\\left(\\sum_{r=1}^3(1-u)^{-r}\\right)^2.\n\\]\nThe function \\(G\\) is increasing, and exact substitution gives\n\\[\nG(.052)&lt;5.583&lt;6.\n\\]\nAlso \\(6u^2\\le u\\) on this interval. If both periodic defects are at most \\(u_j\\), Lemma 2.2 bounds the first output by\n\\[\n6u_j^2=u_{j+1}\\le u_j.\n\\]\nUsing this bound in the second output bounds that output by the same number. This proves the two purity estimates for every \\(j\\ge2\\).</p><p>Each spatial purity exceeds \\(1/2\\), so the maximal spatial modulus is unique and simple. Lemma 2.1 further gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\ne0}\\left|\\frac{\\mu_i(b_j)}{\\lambda_j}\\right|^{n_j}\n\\le d(u_j),\n\\]\nand hence \\(\\rho_j^{n_j}\\le d(u_j)\\). The rational comparison\n\\[\nd(.052)&lt;.971^{120}\n\\]\nproves \\(\\rho_2&lt;.971\\).</p><p>At every level the maximal-modulus eigenvalue is unique. If it were \\(-\\lambda_j\\), then\n\\[\n\\frac{\\operatorname{Tr}(X_{b_j}^n)}{\\lambda_j^n}\\longrightarrow-1\n\\]\nthrough odd integers \\(n\\). Indeed, the residual ratios are bounded by a number below one and their fourth powers have finite sum, so their \\(n\\)th-power sum vanishes. This contradicts positivity of the physical partition function \\(Z_n(b_j)\\) for odd \\(n\\ge5\\). Thus the leading eigenvalue is \\(+\\lambda_j\\).</p><p>Its eigenspace has a real unit vector because \\(X_{b_j}\\) is real. The real unitaries \\(U_g\\) act on this line by signs. The order-three rotation \\(C\\) acts by \\(+1\\). It conjugates the three nonidentity elements of \\(D_2\\) cyclically, so they have the same sign. The product of any two is the third, so the common sign equals its square and is \\(+1\\). Thus \\(\\mathcal G\\) fixes the line pointwise.</p><p>Finally, for even \\(N\\ge4\\), physical squaring gives\n\\[\nZ_N(b_{j+1})\\le Z_N(b_j)^2.\n\\]\nThe residual-tail estimate gives\n\\[\nZ_N(b_j)/\\lambda_j^N=1+o(1),\n\\qquad Z_N(b_j)^{1/N}\\longrightarrow\\lambda_j.\n\\]\nTaking \\(N\\)th roots through even integers yields \\(\\lambda_{j+1}\\le\\lambda_j^2\\).</p><p>For the reverse comparison at the reference scale, \\(M_j=n_{j+1}\\), and the leading spatial probability at \\((M_j,b_{j+1})\\) is at least \\(1-u_{j+1}\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lambda_{j+1}^{M_j}\n&amp;\\ge(1-u_{j+1})Z_{M_j}(b_{j+1})\\\\\n&amp;=(1-u_{j+1})T(M_j,b_j)Z_{M_j}(b_j)^2\\\\\n&amp;\\ge(1-u_{j+1})(1-u_j)\\lambda_j^{2M_j}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThis proves the stated bounds for \\(r_j\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( 1-\\sum_i(w_i')^2\\le f(u) =\\frac{u^2}{2(1-u)^2}. \\) If \\(u<1/2\\), the largest weight is unique and at least \\(1-u\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\((w_i)\\) be a finite or countable probability list.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  \\sum_iw_i^2\\ge1-u,\\qquad 0\\le u&lt;1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Normalize the squares by\n  \\[\n  w_i'=\\frac{w_i^2}{\\sum_kw_k^2}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n1-\\sum_i(w_i')^2\\le f(u)\n=\\frac{u^2}{2(1-u)^2}.\n\\]\nIf \\(u&lt;1/2\\), the largest weight is unique and at least \\(1-u\\). If \\(x\\) is the sum of all other weights, then\n\\[\nx\\le\\frac{1-\\sqrt{1-2u}}2.\n\\]\nIn particular, for \\(0\\le u\\le.1\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{x}{1-x}\\le d(u)=\\frac{u}{2-3u}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Set \\(P=\\sum_iw_i^2\\) and let \\(P'\\) be the purity after squaring. Nonnegativity justifies rearrangement of the countable sums, and\n\\[\n1-P'\n=\\frac{2\\sum_{i&lt;k}w_i^2w_k^2}{P^2}\n\\le\\frac{2(\\sum_{i&lt;k}w_iw_k)^2}{P^2}\n=\\frac{(1-P)^2}{2P^2}\n\\le f(u).\n\\]\nHere \\(f\\) is increasing on \\([0,1)\\).</p><p>A summable nonzero nonnegative list attains its maximum, and\n\\(P\\le\\max_iw_i\\). If \\(u&lt;1/2\\), the maximum therefore exceeds \\(1/2\\) and is unique. Thus \\(x\\le u&lt;1/2\\), and\n\\[\n2x(1-x)\\le1-P\\le u,\n\\]\nwhich gives the square-root bound. Also\n\\[\nx\\le\\frac{u}{2(1-x)}\\le\\frac{u}{2(1-u)}.\n\\]\nApplying the increasing function \\(x\\mapsto x/(1-x)\\) proves the last bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.2; also [16, Lemma 4.2]", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( 1-S(2n,2b)\\le p_+, \\) and \\( 1-T(4n,2b) \\le f\\!\\left(1-t^2(1-p_+)\\right). \\) Either upper defect bound may be increased before subsequent use, provided it stays below one.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\ge4\\) be even and \\(b&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the purities \\(S,T\\) and the function \\(f\\) defined in Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  S(n,b)\\ge s,\\qquad T(2n,b)\\ge t,\\qquad 0&lt;s,t\\le1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  p_+=f(1-s^2t),\n  \\]\n  and suppose \\(p_+&lt;1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n1-S(2n,2b)\\le p_+,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n1-T(4n,2b)\n\\le f\\!\\left(1-t^2(1-p_+)\\right).\n\\]\nEither upper defect bound may be increased before subsequent use, provided it stays below one.</p>", "proof": "<p>Directly from the definitions,\n\\[\nS(n,2b)\n=S(n,b)^2\\frac{T(2n,b)}{T(n,b)^2}\n\\ge s^2t.\n\\]\nApply spatial squaring and Lemma 2.1. A second cancellation gives\n\\[\nT(4n,b)\n=T(2n,b)^2\\frac{S(2n,2b)}{S(2n,b)^2}\n\\ge t^2(1-p_+).\n\\]\nPhysical squaring and Lemma 2.1 prove the second inequality. Monotonicity of \\(f\\) justifies increasing either defect bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Proposition 3.3, “Finite periodic inputs from [16].”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At \\(b_0\\), the spatial eigenvalue list decomposes as one list \\(J\\) and three identical lists \\(N\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the precise spatial operator, shift, spin normalization, and unit bilinear coupling of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  b_0=\\frac{49}{4},\\qquad b_1=2b_0,\n  \\]\n  and use \\(\\ell,U,V,m_J,m_N\\) from Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">The following are imported finite inputs from [16, Corollary 5.6].</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At \\(b_0\\), the spatial eigenvalue list decomposes as one list \\(J\\) and three identical lists \\(N\\). Here \\(J\\) is the spectrum on the subspace fixed by every \\(U_g\\), \\(g\\in D_2\\), and \\(N\\) is the common spectrum on each of the other three simultaneous eigenspaces of these commuting operators.</p><p>In \\(J\\), the absolute eigenvalues are at most \\(U\\), and their twelfth powers sum to at most \\(m_J\\). In each \\(N\\), the absolute eigenvalues are at most \\(V\\), and their twelfth powers sum to at most \\(m_N\\). There is an eigenvalue in \\(J\\) whose modulus exceeds \\(\\ell\\). Furthermore,\n\\[\n1-S(72,b_1)\\le\\frac{151249}{2500000}=.0604996.\n\\]\nFor the periodic chains of \\(72\\) and \\(120\\) sites, the shifted ground energy is strictly negative. Consequently, at each of these lengths and every \\(b&gt;0\\), an individual unnormalized ground Boltzmann weight is greater than one.</p>", "proof": "<p>These are the finite periodic inputs stated in the paper from [16, Corollary 5.6]. Their finite thermal and variational certificates are supplied in that companion source, not proved again in this paper. No boundary Hamiltonian occurs in these inputs.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 4.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( e_{k_{j+1}}(j+1)\\le E_{j+1},\\qquad A(M_{j+1},j+1)\\ge1-\\eta_{j+1}. \\) In particular, the first strict defect bound proves \\(c_{j+1}>0\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the transfer operators and vectors of Proposition 1 and the positive simple leading eigenvalues \\(\\lambda_j\\), residual ratios \\(\\rho_j\\), and periodic bounds of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P_j\\) be the orthogonal projection onto the \\(\\lambda_j\\)-eigenspace, and put\n  \\[\n  c_j=\\|P_jv_{b_j}\\|^2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For even \\(k,m\\ge2\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  B_k(j)&amp;=\\left\\langle v_{b_j},\n  (|X_{b_j}|/\\lambda_j)^kv_{b_j}\\right\\rangle\n  =\\frac{Y_k(b_j)}{\\lambda_j^k}&gt;0,\\\\\n  e_k(j)&amp;=\\frac{B_k(j)-c_j}{B_k(j)},\\\\\n  A(m,j)&amp;=\\frac{Y_m(b_{j+1})}{Y_m(b_j)^2}.\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n  Then \\(0\\le e_k(j)\\le1\\), whether or not \\(c_j=0\\), and \\(0&lt;A(m,j)\\le1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(0&lt;q&lt;1\\), \\(0\\le e&lt;1\\), and \\(0&lt;t\\le1\\), define\n  \\[\n  F(q,e,t)=\\frac{q}{1-q}\n  \\left(\\frac{1}{(1-e)^2t}-1\\right).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(j\\ge2\\), use\n  \\[\n  M_j=240\\,2^{j-2},\\qquad\n  u_2=.052,\\quad u_{j+1}=6u_j^2,\\qquad\n  k_2=142,\\quad k_{j+1}=M_j.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(j\\ge2\\), \\(0\\le E_j,\\eta_j&lt;1\\), and\n  \\[\n  e_{k_j}(j)\\le E_j,\\qquad A(M_j,j)\\ge1-\\eta_j.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E_{j+1},\\eta_{j+1}\\in[0,1)\\) satisfy\n  \\[\n  E_{j+1}\\ge\n  F\\!\\left(d(u_{j+1})^{(M_j-k_j)/M_j},E_j,1-\\eta_j\\right)\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  \\eta_{j+1}\\ge\n  f\\!\\left(\n  1-(1-\\eta_j)(1-u_j)(1-u_{j+1})(1-E_{j+1})\n  \\right).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\ne_{k_{j+1}}(j+1)\\le E_{j+1},\\qquad\nA(M_{j+1},j+1)\\ge1-\\eta_{j+1}.\n\\]\nIn particular, the first strict defect bound proves \\(c_{j+1}&gt;0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Here \\(M_j&gt;k_j\\) and \\(n_{j+1}=M_j\\). Proposition 2 gives\n\\[\n\\rho_{j+1}^{M_j-k_j}\n\\le d(u_{j+1})^{(M_j-k_j)/M_j}&lt;1.\n\\]\nThe function \\(F\\) increases in \\(q,e\\) and decreases in \\(t\\) on the stated domains. Lemma 3.1 proves the first conclusion, including \\(c_{j+1}&gt;0\\).</p><p>Put \\(M=M_j\\). Exact cancellation gives\n\\[\n\\frac{A(2M,j)}{A(M,j)}\n=\nr_j^M\\frac{B_{2M}(j+1)}{B_M(j+1)}\n\\left(\\frac{B_M(j)}{B_{2M}(j)}\\right)^2.\n\\]\nThe squared factor is at least one. The first boundary ratio is at least\n\\[\n\\frac{c_{j+1}}{B_M(j+1)}\\ge1-E_{j+1}.\n\\]\nThus Proposition 2 yields\n\\[\nA(2M,j)\\ge\n(1-\\eta_j)(1-u_j)(1-u_{j+1})(1-E_{j+1}).\n\\]</p><p>At the fixed physical length \\(2M\\), passing from \\(b_j\\) to \\(b_{j+1}\\) normalizes the squares of the Gibbs probabilities. Lemma 2.1 bounds the resulting defect by \\(f\\) of the preceding defect. Since \\(M_{j+1}=2M\\), this is the asserted bound for \\(A(M_{j+1},j+1)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( e_m(j+1)\\le F(q,e_k(j),A(m,j)). \\) An upper bound strictly below one on the right proves \\(c_{j+1}>0\\) without presupposing it.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(B_k(j)\\), \\(e_k(j)\\), \\(c_j\\), and \\(A(m,j)\\) from Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(j\\ge0\\), let \\(m&gt;k\\ge2\\) be even, and suppose \\(e_k(j)&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(0&lt;q&lt;1\\) satisfy\n  \\[\n  q\\ge\\rho_{j+1}^{m-k}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\ne_m(j+1)\\le F(q,e_k(j),A(m,j)).\n\\]\nAn upper bound strictly below one on the right proves \\(c_{j+1}&gt;0\\) without presupposing it.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(D_k(j)=B_k(j)-c_j\\). The spectral theorem gives\n\\[\n0\\le D_m(j)\\le\\rho_j^{m-k}D_k(j)\\qquad(m\\ge k),\n\\]\nand \\(B_m(j)\\le B_k(j)\\).</p><p>At the new temperature,\n\\[\nB_m(j+1)-c_{j+1}\n\\le q[B_k(j+1)-c_{j+1}].\n\\]\nSubtract and rearrange:\n\\[\ne_m(j+1)\\le\n\\frac{q}{1-q}\n\\left(\\frac{B_k(j+1)}{B_m(j+1)}-1\\right).\n\\]\nWith \\(r_j=\\lambda_{j+1}/\\lambda_j^2\\), the definitions give the exact identity\n\\[\n\\frac{B_k(j+1)}{B_m(j+1)}\n=\n\\frac{A(k,j)}{A(m,j)}\n\\left(\\frac{B_k(j)}{B_m(j)}\\right)^2r_j^{m-k}.\n\\]\nProposition 2 gives \\(r_j\\le1\\), and physical purity gives \\(A(k,j)\\le1\\). Since \\(e_k(j)&lt;1\\), one has \\(c_j&gt;0\\), and \\(B_m(j)\\ge c_j\\) gives\n\\[\n\\frac{B_k(j)}{B_m(j)}\n\\le(1-e_k(j))^{-1}.\n\\]\nSubstitution proves the inequality. Only the old overlap \\(c_j\\) has been divided by. The new overlap follows from \\(e_m(j+1)&lt;1\\) and \\(B_m(j+1)&gt;0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Proposition A.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( Y_6(b_0)<.027,\\qquad c_0P_*(\\lambda_0)^2<.003745. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  h=\\frac35,\\qquad a=\\frac{700741}{500000},\\qquad b_0=\\frac{49}{4}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\lambda_0\\) be the positive leading eigenvalue of \\(X_{b_0}\\), and let\n  \\[\n  c_0=\\|P_0v_{b_0}\\|^2\n  \\]\n  for the orthogonal projection \\(P_0\\) onto its eigenspace.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  P_*(x)=x^3-.14x^2-.18x.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(2\\le n\\le6\\) and \\(g\\in\\{1,-1,0\\}\\), define\n  \\[\n  H_{n,g}=\\sum_{r=0}^{n-2}S_r\\cdot S_{r+1}\n  -\\frac35S_0^z+\\frac35R_g,\n  \\qquad\n  R_g=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  gS_{n-1}^z,&amp;g=1,-1,\\\\\n  S_{n-1}^x,&amp;g=0,\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  Z_{n,g}=\\operatorname{Tr}\\exp[-b_0(H_{n,g}+anI)].\n  \\]\n  Thus \\(Z_{n,1}=Y_n(b_0)\\) and \\(Z_{n,-1}=Y_n^{\\mathrm{s}}(b_0)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\Pi=\\frac1{|\\mathcal G|}\\sum_{\\gamma\\in\\mathcal G}U_\\gamma,\n  \\qquad\n  I(n)=\\langle v_{b_0},X_{b_0}^n\\Pi v_{b_0}\\rangle,\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\mathcal G\\) is the twelve-element rotation group of Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nY_6(b_0)&lt;.027,\\qquad\nc_0P_*(\\lambda_0)^2&lt;.003745.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The group \\(\\mathcal G\\) has order twelve, and its orbit of \\(z\\) is the six signed coordinate axes, each with the same multiplicity. Proposition 1 writes \\(I(n)\\) as the average of the corresponding right-field partition functions. Global rotations about \\(z\\) identify the four perpendicular orientations. Hence\n\\[\nI(n)=\\frac{Z_{n,1}+Z_{n,-1}+4Z_{n,0}}6.\n\\]</p><p>The averaging operator \\(\\Pi\\) is an orthogonal projection commuting with \\(X_{b_0}\\). Proposition 2 says that \\(\\mathcal G\\) fixes the leading line pointwise. Therefore\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nc_0P_*(\\lambda_0)^2\n&amp;\\le\\|P_*(X_{b_0})\\Pi v_{b_0}\\|^2\\\\\n&amp;=\\langle v_{b_0},P_*(X_{b_0})^2\\Pi v_{b_0}\\rangle\\\\\n&amp;=I(6)-.28I(5)-.3404I(4)+.0504I(3)+.0324I(2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nNo positivity of the individual odd powers is assumed. It remains to enclose the fifteen short-chain traces.</p><p>The matrix recurrence of Lemma 4.1 has the following scalar specification. Number words by \\(\\sum_j(w_j+1)3^j\\), if desired, and interpret out-of-range lookups as zero. Initialize \\(x(w,r)=0\\). At stage \\(i=K,\\ldots,0\\), form\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nz(w,r)\n={}&amp;\\eta(w)x(w,r)\n-10\\sum_{j=0}^{n-2}\\sum_{\\sigma=\\pm1}\nx(w+\\sigma(e_j-e_{j+1}),r)\\\\\n&amp;-\\mathbf1_{\\{g=0\\}}3(1+p_w)\n\\sum_{\\sigma=\\pm1}x(w+\\sigma e_{n-1},r),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nand replace all entries simultaneously by\n\\[\nx_{\\mathrm{new}}(w,r)\n=t_i\\mathbf1_{\\{w=r\\}}+\\left\\lfloor\\frac{z(w,r)}{80}\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nAt the end,\n\\[\nk_{n,g}:=\\lfloor10^{16}W_{n,g}\\rfloor\n=\n\\left\\lfloor\n\\frac{10^{16}\\sum_{w,r}x(w,r)^2(2-p_w)(1+p_r)}{2Q^2}\n\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nThe weight follows from\n\\[\n\\frac{1+p_r}{1+p_w}=\\frac{(2-p_w)(1+p_r)}2.\n\\]\nAll these operations are integer operations. The resulting fifteen entries are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|rrr}\nn\\backslash g&amp;1&amp;-1&amp;0\\\\ \\hline\n2&amp;10185296075298&amp;4160017987&amp;583578389575\\\\\n3&amp;259254948723&amp;571111660440989&amp;47796720049308\\\\\n4&amp;318278534893&amp;661347985&amp;43971049724\\\\\n5&amp;260908126900&amp;29917222640283&amp;5659378873682\\\\\n6&amp;16467340956&amp;328219854&amp;4086161507\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe displayed recurrence provides a finite exact-arithmetic verification of every entry.</p><p>Since \\(E=\\exp[-b_0(H_{n,g}+C_nI)/2]\\) is self-adjoint,\n\\[\n\\|E\\|_F^2=\\operatorname{Tr}\\exp[-b_0(H_{n,g}+C_nI)].\n\\]\nLemma 4.1 and\n\\[\n|\\|E\\|_F^2-\\|\\widehat E\\|_F^2|\n\\le2\\|E-\\widehat E\\|_F\\|\\widehat E\\|_F+\\|E-\\widehat E\\|_F^2\n\\]\ngive\n\\[\n\\left|Z_{n,g}-e^{b_0(C_n-an)}W_{n,g}\\right|\n&lt;\ne^{b_0(C_n-an)}\n\\left(4\\cdot10^{-10}\\sqrt{W_{n,g}}+4\\cdot10^{-20}\\right).\n\\]</p><p>For a printed entry \\(k=k_{n,g}\\), put\n\\[\nL=\\frac{k}{10^{16}},\\qquad\nU=\\frac{k+1}{10^{16}},\\qquad\nr=\\frac{\\lfloor\\sqrt{k+1}\\rfloor+1}{10^8},\\qquad\n\\epsilon=4\\cdot10^{-10}r+4\\cdot10^{-20}.\n\\]\nThen \\(L\\le W_{n,g}&lt;U\\), \\(\\sqrt{W_{n,g}}&lt;r\\), and \\(L-\\epsilon&gt;0\\). Therefore\n\\[\ne^{b_0(C_n-an)}(L-\\epsilon)\n&lt;Z_{n,g}&lt;\ne^{b_0(C_n-an)}(U+\\epsilon).\n\\]\nNo unprinted digits of \\(W_{n,g}\\) are used.</p><p>For \\(0\\le y\\le10\\), set \\(T_{80}(y)=\\sum_{j=0}^{80}y^j/j!\\). Comparison of consecutive tail terms gives\n\\[\nT_{80}(y)\\le e^y\\le T_{80}(y)+\\frac{2y^{81}}{81!}.\n\\]\nFor negative arguments, take reciprocals and reverse endpoints. All arguments \\(b_0(C_n-an)\\) lie between \\(-.0544635\\) and \\(9.691073\\). Thus only rational arithmetic and integer square roots are required.</p><p>In particular,\n\\[\n.0266319440&lt;Z_{6,1}&lt;.0266319607&lt;.027.\n\\]\nThe same intervals give the following rational centers for \\(I(n)\\), each with absolute error less than \\(10^{-7}\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|ccccc}\nn&amp;2&amp;3&amp;4&amp;5&amp;6\\\\ \\hline\n\\text{center of }I(n)&amp;.02703074&amp;.01203562&amp;.01193511&amp;.00931560&amp;.00893272\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nSubstitution in the polynomial combination gives \\(.003744031780\\). The sum of the absolute polynomial coefficients is \\(1.7032\\), so the total possible error is less than \\(1.7032\\cdot10^{-7}\\). Consequently,\n\\[\nc_0P_*(\\lambda_0)^2\n&lt;\n.003744031780+.00000017032\n=.0037442021&lt;.003745.\n\\]\nTogether with the bound on \\(Z_{6,1}=Y_6(b_0)\\), this proves the proposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma A.2, with the approximation data from Sections A.2–A.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For each of the fifteen pairs \\((n,g)\\), \\( \\left\\|\\widehat E-\\exp[-b_0(H_{n,g}+C_nI)/2]\\right\\|_F <2\\cdot10^{-10}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(H_{n,g}\\), \\(2\\le n\\le6\\), \\(g\\in\\{1,-1,0\\}\\), from Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  C_n=3\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rfloor\n  +2((n-1)\\bmod2)+\\frac65,\\qquad\n  A=I-\\frac{H_{n,g}+C_nI}{8}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Index the spin-weight basis by words \\(w=(w_0,\\ldots,w_{n-1})\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}^n\\), and let\n  \\[\n  p_w=\\left(\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}w_j\\right)\\bmod2\\in\\{0,1\\},\n  \\qquad J_{ww}=\\sqrt{1+p_w},\\qquad D=80JAJ^{-1}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The diagonal of the integral matrix \\(D\\) is\n  \\[\n  \\eta(w)=80-10C_n-10\\sum_{j=0}^{n-2}w_jw_{j+1}\n  +6w_0-6gw_{n-1}.\n  \\]\n  Each allowed bond hop \\(w\\leftrightarrow w+\\sigma(e_j-e_{j+1})\\), \\(\\sigma=\\pm1\\), has entry \\(-10\\). When \\(g=0\\), an allowed end hop from column \\(r=w+\\sigma e_{n-1}\\) to row \\(w\\) has entry \\(-3(1+p_w)\\). These are all entries of \\(D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  K=192,\\qquad Q=2^{50},\\qquad\n  s_i=\\frac{49^iK!}{i!},\\qquad\n  S_K=\\sum_{i=0}^Ks_i,\\qquad\n  t_i=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{Qs_i}{S_K}\\right\\rfloor.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Starting with \\(\\mathcal X_{K+1}=0\\), compute\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal X_i=\n  \\left\\lfloor\\frac{D\\mathcal X_{i+1}}{80}\\right\\rfloor+t_iI,\n  \\qquad i=K,K-1,\\ldots,0.\n  \\]\n  Matrix floors are entrywise, always toward \\(-\\infty\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\widehat E=J^{-1}\\frac{\\mathcal X_0}{Q}J,\\qquad\n  W_{n,g}=\\|\\widehat E\\|_F^2,\n  \\qquad \\|M\\|_F^2=\\operatorname{Tr}(M^*M).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For each of the fifteen pairs \\((n,g)\\),\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\widehat E-\\exp[-b_0(H_{n,g}+C_nI)/2]\\right\\|_F\n&lt;2\\cdot10^{-10}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>First, \\(A\\) is a self-adjoint contraction. A single bond has eigenvalues \\(-2,-1,1\\). For two consecutive bonds, couple the outside spins to spin \\(t\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\), and then couple this with the middle spin to total spin \\(j\\). The operator\n\\[\nS_1\\cdot S_2+S_2\\cdot S_3=S_2\\cdot(S_1+S_3)\n\\]\nhas eigenvalues\n\\[\n\\frac{j(j+1)-t(t+1)-2}{2},\\qquad |t-1|\\le j\\le t+1.\n\\]\nIts minimum is \\(-3\\). Pairing consecutive bonds, with one single bond left over if necessary, and using the total boundary-field norm \\(6/5\\), gives\n\\[\n0\\le H_{n,g}+C_nI\n\\le(n-1+6/5+C_n)I\\le16I.\n\\]\nAdding the lower bounds for paired bonds is valid even where their three-site supports overlap. For \\(n=2,\\ldots,6\\), the upper bounds are \\(5.4,7.4,10.4,12.4,15.4\\). Thus \\(-I\\le A\\le I\\); positivity of \\(A\\) itself is not required.</p><p>The stated entries of \\(D\\) follow directly from the spin-weight basis. Bond hops preserve \\(p_w\\), and the corresponding bond entry is \\(1\\), giving \\(-10\\). For \\(g=0\\), the end-field entry before similarity is \\(3\\sqrt2/10\\), and opposite parities give\n\\[\n-10\\frac{3\\sqrt2}{10}\n\\sqrt{\\frac{1+p_w}{1+p_r}}\n=-3(1+p_w).\n\\]\nThus \\(D\\) is integral.</p><p>Write \\(d=3^n\\). Since \\(b_0/2=49/8\\), the exact target is\n\\[\nE=e^{-49}\\exp(49A)\n=\\sum_{i=0}^\\infty e^{-49}\\frac{49^i}{i!}A^i.\n\\]\nThe polynomial with coefficients \\(q_i=s_i/S_K\\) is the truncation of this Poisson expansion, renormalized to coefficient sum one. Its coefficient \\(\\ell^1\\) error is twice the omitted Poisson mass, at most \\(2R\\), where\n\\[\nR=\\frac{49^{193}}{193!}\\frac1{1-49/194}\n&lt;3.152\\cdot10^{-33}.\n\\]\nThe factor \\(e^{-49}\\) has been discarded in this upper bound. Since \\(A\\) is a contraction, the corresponding Frobenius error is at most \\(2\\sqrt d\\,R\\).</p><p>At each Horner stage, replacing \\(q_i\\) by \\(t_i/Q\\) contributes less than \\(\\sqrt d/Q\\) in Frobenius norm. The entrywise floor contributes less than \\(d/Q\\) before undoing the similarity and hence less than \\(\\sqrt2\\,d/Q\\) afterward, since\n\\[\n\\|J^{-1}\\|\\|J\\|\\le\\sqrt2.\n\\]\nSubsequent propagation is left multiplication by \\(A\\), which cannot increase Frobenius norm. Thus the contraction is used after undoing the similarity; no contraction property of \\(D\\) is asserted.</p><p>The total error is less than\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sqrt d\\,R+\\frac{193(\\sqrt d+\\sqrt2\\,d)}Q\n&amp;\\le54R+\\frac{193(27+\\sqrt2\\,729)}{2^{50}}\\\\\n&amp;&lt;54R+\\frac{432513}{2^{51}}\n&lt;2\\cdot10^{-10}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe penultimate inequality uses \\(\\sqrt2&lt;3/2\\); the final inequality is a rational comparison using the displayed bound on \\(R\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Proposition B.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( E_0^{\\mathrm{opp}}(m)+am<\\alpha \\qquad\\text{for }m\\in\\{34,78,142,240\\}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  a=\\frac{700741}{500000},\\qquad h=\\frac35,\\qquad\n  \\alpha=\\frac{2021}{5000}=.4042.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">On \\(m\\) sites labeled \\(1,\\ldots,m\\), define\n  \\[\n  H_m^{\\mathrm{opp}}\n  =\\sum_{j=1}^{m-1}S_j\\cdot S_{j+1}\n  -\\frac35S_1^z+\\frac35S_m^z.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E_0^{\\mathrm{opp}}(m)\\) be its smallest eigenvalue.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nE_0^{\\mathrm{opp}}(m)+am&lt;\\alpha\n\\qquad\\text{for }m\\in\\{34,78,142,240\\}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For each of the four lengths, Lemma 5.2 gives\n\\[\nV_m=\\langle\\psi_m,\\psi_m\\rangle&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe variational principle, Lemma 5.1, and the strict integer comparison of Lemma 5.2 yield\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_0^{\\mathrm{opp}}(m)+am\n&amp;\\le\n\\frac{\\langle\\psi_m,(H_m^{\\mathrm{opp}}+amI)\\psi_m\\rangle}\n{\\langle\\psi_m,\\psi_m\\rangle}\\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{H_m^*}{500000V_m}\n&lt;\\frac{202100}{500000}\n=\\alpha.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAll four inequalities use the same fixed field \\(h=3/5\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma B.2; tensor and endpoint data from Section B.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( V_m=L_mu, \\) and \\( \\begin{aligned} H_m^*={}& 500000\\sum_{r=0}^{m-2}L_0T_*^rO_*T_*^{m-2-r}u\\\\ &-300000L_0G_*T_*^{m-1}u +300000L_{m-1}G_*u +700741mL_mu. \\end{aligned} \\) In particular, \\(V_m\\) and \\(H_m^*\\) are …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(H_m^{\\mathrm{opp}}\\) and \\(a\\) from Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal V\\) have the \\(26\\) labels\n  \\[\n  (i,d),\\qquad\n  1\\le i\\le8,\\qquad\n  d=-\\ell_i,-\\ell_i+2,\\ldots,\\ell_i,\n  \\qquad\n  (\\ell_i)_{i=1}^8=(1,1,1,1,3,3,3,5),\n  \\]\n  ordered first by \\(i\\), then by increasing \\(d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define real \\(26\\times26\\) matrices \\(A_s\\), \\(s\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}\\), as follows. At row \\((i,d)\\) and column \\((j,e)\\), put\n  \\[\n  \\ell=\\ell_i,\\qquad k=\\ell_j,\\qquad r=\\frac{\\ell+d}{2}.\n  \\]\n  The entry is zero unless\n  \\[\n  e=d+2s,\\qquad |k-\\ell|\\le2,\\qquad k=\\ell\\Longrightarrow i=j.\n  \\]\n  For an allowed entry, multiply the coefficient in the second column below by the factor in its \\(s\\)-column:\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c|ccc}\n  &amp;\\text{coefficient}&amp;s=-1&amp;s=0&amp;s=1\\\\ \\hline\n  k=\\ell+2&amp;P_{ij}&amp;2(\\ell+1-r)(\\ell+2-r)&amp;2(r+1)(\\ell-r+1)&amp;(r+1)(r+2)\\\\\n  k=\\ell&amp;D_i&amp;2(\\ell-r+1)&amp;d&amp;-(r+1)\\\\\n  k=\\ell-2&amp;-P_{ji}&amp;2&amp;-2&amp;1\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n  Out-of-range columns are absent.</p><p class=\"li\">The parameters are\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|rrrr}\n  i&amp;\\ell_i&amp;D_i&amp;\\beta_i&amp;\\kappa_i\\\\ \\hline\n  1&amp;1&amp;-289&amp;10000&amp;12\\\\\n  2&amp;1&amp;-84&amp;5620&amp;12\\\\\n  3&amp;1&amp;-9&amp;2456&amp;12\\\\\n  4&amp;1&amp;33&amp;4868&amp;12\\\\\n  5&amp;3&amp;-22&amp;82&amp;6\\\\\n  6&amp;3&amp;27&amp;-809&amp;6\\\\\n  7&amp;3&amp;71&amp;12475&amp;6\\\\\n  8&amp;5&amp;-11&amp;-9645&amp;1\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n  and the fifteen couplings, defined when \\(\\ell_j=\\ell_i+2\\), are\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|r@{\\qquad}c|r@{\\qquad}c|r}\n  (i,j)&amp;P_{ij}&amp;(i,j)&amp;P_{ij}&amp;(i,j)&amp;P_{ij}\\\\ \\hline\n  (1,5)&amp;-11&amp;(2,7)&amp;-49&amp;(4,6)&amp;9\\\\\n  (1,6)&amp;-35&amp;(3,5)&amp;-6&amp;(4,7)&amp;-32\\\\\n  (1,7)&amp;-28&amp;(3,6)&amp;-25&amp;(5,8)&amp;1\\\\\n  (2,5)&amp;-23&amp;(3,7)&amp;-20&amp;(6,8)&amp;2\\\\\n  (2,6)&amp;16&amp;(4,5)&amp;-34&amp;(7,8)&amp;-16\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define endpoint columns by\n  \\[\n  b_{(i,d)}=\n  \\begin{cases}\\beta_i,&amp;d=-1,\\\\0,&amp;d\\ne-1,\\end{cases}\n  \\qquad\n  c_{(i,d)}=\n  \\begin{cases}\\kappa_i\\beta_i,&amp;d=-1,\\\\0,&amp;d\\ne-1.\\end{cases}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(m\\ge2\\), define the physical vector\n  \\[\n  \\psi_m=\n  \\sum_{s_1,\\ldots,s_m=-1}^{1}\n  (b^tA_{s_1}\\cdots A_{s_m}c)\\,\n  e_{s_1}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes e_{s_m}.\n  \\]\n  These are ordinary real amplitudes in the orthonormal physical basis.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  B_{st}=A_sA_t,\\qquad\n  T_*=\\sum_{s=-1}^1A_s\\otimes A_s,\\qquad\n  G_*=\\sum_{s=-1}^1sA_s\\otimes A_s,\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  O_*={}&amp;\\sum_{s,t=-1}^1st\\,B_{st}\\otimes B_{st}\\\\\n  &amp;+\\sum_{\\substack{s=-1,0\\\\t=0,1}}\n  \\left(B_{st}\\otimes B_{s+1,t-1}\n  +B_{s+1,t-1}\\otimes B_{st}\\right).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n  Use the usual Kronecker convention\n  \\[\n  (C\\otimes D)_{(v,w),(v',w')}=C_{v,v'}D_{w,w'}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  L_0=(b\\otimes b)^t,\\qquad u=c\\otimes c,\\qquad L_i=L_0T_*^i,\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  V_m=\\langle\\psi_m,\\psi_m\\rangle,\\qquad\n  H_m^*=500000\\langle\\psi_m,(H_m^{\\mathrm{opp}}+amI)\\psi_m\\rangle.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nV_m=L_mu,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_m^*={}&amp;\n500000\\sum_{r=0}^{m-2}L_0T_*^rO_*T_*^{m-2-r}u\\\\\n&amp;-300000L_0G_*T_*^{m-1}u\n+300000L_{m-1}G_*u\n+700741mL_mu.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(V_m\\) and \\(H_m^*\\) are integers.</p>", "proof": "<p>The support rule forces a nonzero amplitude to have \\(\\sum_js_j=0\\), because both endpoints have virtual weight \\(-1\\). This restriction does not restrict the variational upper bound on the smallest eigenvalue of the full Hamiltonian. Normalization of the matrices is unnecessary; no virtual inner product or physical basis weights are inserted.</p><p>Expanding \\(T_*^m\\) sums the products of two identical real amplitudes over all physical words. This proves the norm formula directly in the orthonormal physical basis. Inserting \\(G_*\\) at one site instead of \\(T_*\\) multiplies the summand by the weight \\(s\\), and therefore inserts \\(S^z\\).</p><p>For a bond, the spin normalization gives\n\\[\nS\\cdot S=S^z\\otimes S^z\n+\\frac12(S^+\\otimes S^-+S^-\\otimes S^+).\n\\]\nIts diagonal entry on \\((s,t)\\) is \\(st\\). Its off-diagonal entries are \\(1\\) between \\((s,t)\\) and \\((s+1,t-1)\\), for \\(s\\in\\{-1,0\\}\\), \\(t\\in\\{0,1\\}\\), since each allowed ladder entry is \\(\\sqrt2\\). The two summands in each parenthesis defining \\(O_*\\) give the two directions of that matrix element. Thus \\(O_*\\) inserts exactly one unit-coupling bond, with no extra factor or metric. Its position after \\(r\\) sites gives the \\(r\\)th summand.</p><p>The left and right endpoint coefficients are\n\\[\n500000(-3/5)=-300000,\\qquad\n500000(3/5)=300000.\n\\]\nThe constant shift contributes \\(500000am=700741m\\) times the norm. There are \\(m-1\\) bond positions and no closing bond. Every matrix, row, and column in the formula is integral.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Sections B.3–B.5, including Table 4 and the exact integer comparisons", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The restrictions preserve every contraction in Lemma 5.1, and \\( \\dim\\mathcal K_0=162. \\) The exact row recurrence gives \\( V_m=L_mu,\\qquad H_m^*=(N_m+300000L_{m-1}G_*+700741L_m)u. \\) For \\(m\\in\\{34,78,142,240\\}\\), the …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the tensors, endpoints, matrices, and quantities of Lemma 5.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal Q=\\{((i,d),(j,d)):(i,d),(j,d)\\in\\mathcal V\\}\n  \\]\n  and let \\(\\mathcal K_0\\) be its coordinate subspace. Restrict \\(T_*,G_*,O_*,L_0,u\\) to this subspace, in inherited lexicographic order.</p><p class=\"li\">Define integer rows\n  \\[\n  L_i=L_{i-1}T_*\\quad(i\\ge1),\\qquad\n  N_1=-300000L_0G_*,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  N_i=N_{i-1}T_*+\n  L_{i-2}(500000O_*+700741T_*^2)\\quad(i\\ge2).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For each required length, define \\(p_m,q_m,k_m\\) by the following table:\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|rrr}\n  m&amp;p_m&amp;q_m&amp;k_m\\\\ \\hline\n  34&amp;202&amp;715867&amp;4031974\\\\\n  78&amp;440&amp;747265&amp;4041175\\\\\n  142&amp;787&amp;121288&amp;4041110\\\\\n  240&amp;1317&amp;165352&amp;4041006\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The restrictions preserve every contraction in Lemma 5.1, and\n\\[\n\\dim\\mathcal K_0=162.\n\\]\nThe exact row recurrence gives\n\\[\nV_m=L_mu,\\qquad\nH_m^*=(N_m+300000L_{m-1}G_*+700741L_m)u.\n\\]\nFor \\(m\\in\\{34,78,142,240\\}\\), the exact integer outputs satisfy\n\\[\n10^{p_m-1}\\le V_m&lt;10^{p_m},\n\\]\n\\[\nq_m10^{p_m-6}\\le V_m&lt;(q_m+1)10^{p_m-6},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nk_mV_m\\le20H_m^*&lt;(k_m+1)V_m.\n\\]\nIn particular,\n\\[\nV_m&gt;0,\\qquad 202100V_m-H_m^*&gt;0\n\\]\nat all four lengths.</p>", "proof": "<p>On a paired virtual label \\(((i,d),(j,e))\\), consider the difference \\(d-e\\). Each nonzero entry of \\(A_s\\) changes virtual weight by \\(2s\\) from row to column. Hence each summand \\(A_s\\otimes A_s\\) of \\(T_*\\) or \\(G_*\\) preserves this difference.</p><p>A nonzero entry of \\(B_{st}\\) changes weight by \\(2(s+t)\\). The two factors in every tensor summand of \\(O_*\\) have the same total physical weight, including the pair \\((s,t)\\) and \\((s+1,t-1)\\). Thus each summand preserves \\(d-e\\).</p><p>The multiplicities of \\(d=-5,-3,-1,1,3,5\\) are \\(1,4,8,8,4,1\\), respectively, so\n\\[\n\\dim\\mathcal K_0=1^2+4^2+8^2+8^2+4^2+1^2=162.\n\\]\nBoth endpoint tensors are supported in \\(\\mathcal K_0\\). The three matrices are block diagonal by weight difference, so restricting before multiplication or taking powers preserves all contractions. In particular, squaring the restricted \\(T_*\\) gives the restriction of \\(T_*^2\\); no contribution passes through a discarded state.</p><p>Induction in the row recurrence gives\n\\[\nN_m=-300000L_0G_*T_*^{m-1}\n+\\sum_{r=0}^{m-2}\nL_0T_*^r(500000O_*+700741T_*^2)T_*^{m-2-r}.\n\\]\nIts bond contributions and left field are exactly those of Lemma 5.1. Each of its \\(m-1\\) shift contributions equals \\(700741L_m\\). The final \\(700741L_m\\) supplies the remaining site shift, and the other final term supplies the right field with positive sign.</p><p>The whole contraction can be implemented with three rows. Initialize\n\\[\nx=L_0,\\qquad y=L_0T_*,\\qquad w=-300000L_0G_*.\n\\]\nFor \\(m=2,\\ldots,240\\), make simultaneous updates using the old rows:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nw_{\\mathrm{new}}&amp;=wT_*+500000xO_*+700741(xT_*)T_*,\\\\\nx_{\\mathrm{new}}&amp;=y,\\qquad y_{\\mathrm{new}}=yT_*.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAfter this iteration,\n\\[\n(x,y,w)=(L_{m-1},L_m,N_m).\n\\]\nAt a required length compute\n\\[\nV=yu,\\qquad H=wu+300000x(G_*u)+700741V.\n\\]\nThen \\((V,H)=(V_m,H_m^*)\\). The initialization realizes the same invariant at \\(m=1\\), proving the indexing. All operations are exact integer matrix multiplications.</p><p>The integer outputs stated in the lemma give\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nm&amp;H_m^*/(500000V_m)\\text{ lies in}\\\\ \\hline\n34&amp;[.4031974,.4031975)\\\\\n78&amp;[.4041175,.4041176)\\\\\n142&amp;[.4041110,.4041111)\\\\\n240&amp;[.4041006,.4041007)\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nIndeed \\(10^7/500000=20\\), so\n\\[\nk_m=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{10^7H_m^*}{500000V_m}\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nEach displayed \\(k_m+1\\) is less than\n\\[\n4042000=20\\cdot202100.\n\\]\nThus \\(202100V_m-H_m^*&gt;0\\), while the norm-size comparison proves \\(V_m&gt;0\\).</p><p>The paper records the full integer outputs in\n\\(\\texttt{verification/trial-exact-results.json}\\), the matrices and paired labels in\n\\(\\texttt{verification/trial-exact-matrices.json}\\), and the recurrence implementation in\n\\(\\texttt{verification/trial\\_verify.py}\\). The equations and tensor tables specify the calculation independently of these files.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 5.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( E_0(H_m^{\\mathrm{opp}})+am<\\alpha \\qquad(m=34,78,142,240), \\) and, at \\(b_0=49/4\\), \\( Y_6(b_0)<.027,\\qquad c_0P_*(\\lambda_0)^2<.003745. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the opposite-field Hamiltonians and transfer quantities of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  \\alpha=.4042,\\qquad P_*(x)=x^3-.14x^2-.18x.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\lambda_0\\) and \\(c_0\\) be the leading eigenvalue and squared boundary overlap defined in Propositions 2–3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nE_0(H_m^{\\mathrm{opp}})+am&lt;\\alpha\n\\qquad(m=34,78,142,240),\n\\]\nand, at \\(b_0=49/4\\),\n\\[\nY_6(b_0)&lt;.027,\\qquad c_0P_*(\\lambda_0)^2&lt;.003745.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 5 proves the four variational inequalities with explicit integer trial vectors. Proposition 4 proves the two thermal inequalities by finite thermal recurrences with certified rounding errors. These are precisely the finite boundary data required for initialization.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Proposition 5.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(j\\ge5\\), \\( u_j\\le x_j,\\qquad e_{k_j}(j)\\le x_j,\\qquad 1-A(M_j,j)\\le x_j. \\) Moreover, \\( x_j=10^{-1-2^{j-5}}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(X_{b_j}\\), \\(v_{b_j}\\), \\(\\lambda_j\\), \\(P_j\\), and \\(Y_m\\) from Propositions 1–3, where\n  \\[\n  b_j=2^j\\frac{49}{4},\\qquad c_j=\\|P_jv_{b_j}\\|^2,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  B_k(j)=\\langle v_{b_j},(|X_{b_j}|/\\lambda_j)^kv_{b_j}\\rangle,\n  \\qquad e_k(j)=\\frac{B_k(j)-c_j}{B_k(j)},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  A(m,j)=\\frac{Y_m(b_{j+1})}{Y_m(b_j)^2}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(j\\ge2\\), set\n  \\[\n  M_j=240\\,2^{j-2},\\qquad\n  k_2=142,\\quad k_{j+1}=M_j,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  u_2=.052,\\qquad u_{j+1}=6u_j^2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  x_5=.01,\\qquad x_{j+1}=10x_j^2\\quad(j\\ge5).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(j\\ge5\\),\n\\[\nu_j\\le x_j,\\qquad\ne_{k_j}(j)\\le x_j,\\qquad\n1-A(M_j,j)\\le x_j.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\nx_j=10^{-1-2^{j-5}}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 7.2 proves the two boundary inequalities at \\(j=5\\), and\n\\[\nu_5&lt;.000015&lt;.01=x_5.\n\\]\nEnlarge all three bounds at \\(j=5\\) to \\(.01\\).</p><p>Suppose the three bounds hold at level \\(j\\), and write \\(x=x_j\\le.01\\). Here \\(k_j=M_j/2\\), and\n\\[\nu_{j+1}=6u_j^2\\le6x^2.\n\\]\nThe first argument of \\(F\\) in Proposition 3 satisfies\n\\[\nq=\\sqrt{d(u_{j+1})}\n\\le\\sqrt{\\frac{6x^2}{2-18x^2}}\\le2x.\n\\]\nThus the first output is bounded by \\(F(2x,x,1-x)\\). For \\(x&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{F(2x,x,1-x)}{x^2}\n=\n\\frac2{1-2x}\n\\left((1-x)^{-1}+(1-x)^{-2}+(1-x)^{-3}\\right).\n\\]\nAfter continuous extension at zero, this expression is increasing on \\([0,.01]\\). Its endpoint value is\n\\[\n\\frac{42430000}{6792093}&lt;10.\n\\]\nHence take \\(E_{j+1}=10x^2\\).</p><p>For factors in \\([0,1]\\), the defect of their product is at most the sum of the defects. The four-factor product in Proposition 3 has defect at most\n\\[\nx+x+6x^2+10x^2=2x+16x^2.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\frac{f(2x+16x^2)}{x^2}\n=\n\\frac{(2+16x)^2}{2(1-2x-16x^2)^2}\n\\]\nis increasing on \\([0,.01]\\), with endpoint value\n\\[\n\\frac{3645000}{1495729}&lt;10.\n\\]\nThus take \\(\\eta_{j+1}=10x^2\\). Finally,\n\\[\nu_{j+1}\\le6x^2\\le10x^2.\n\\]\nProposition 3 proves the three bounds at the next level. The sequence remains in \\([0,.01]\\). Solving\n\\[\nx_5=.01,\\qquad x_{j+1}=10x_j^2\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\nx_j=10^{-1-2^{j-5}}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At these four lengths, \\( e_m(0)\\le\\epsilon(m), \\qquad A(m,j)\\ge\\left(1+R(m)^{2^j}\\right)^{-2} \\quad(j\\ge0). \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the finite boundary data of Proposition 6, with\n  \\[\n  \\alpha=.4042,\\qquad b_0=\\frac{49}{4},\\qquad\n  \\ell=.999,\\qquad U=1.00139.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(m\\in\\{34,78,142,240\\}\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\epsilon(m)=\n  .027e^{b_0\\alpha}U^m\\frac{.874^{m-6}}{\\ell^6},\n  \\qquad\n  R(m)=e^{.132}U^m+\\epsilon(m)-1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(e_m(0)\\) and \\(A(m,j)\\) as defined in Proposition 7.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At these four lengths,\n\\[\ne_m(0)\\le\\epsilon(m),\n\\qquad\nA(m,j)\\ge\\left(1+R(m)^{2^j}\\right)^{-2}\n\\quad(j\\ge0).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>By the residual spectral bound, \\(\\rho_0\\le.874\\), and \\(\\ell&lt;\\lambda_0\\le U\\),\n\\[\n\\lambda_0^m(B_m(0)-c_0)\n\\le\nU^m.874^{m-6}\\frac{Y_6(b_0)}{\\ell^6}\n&lt;\ne^{-b_0\\alpha}\\epsilon(m).\n\\]\nThe trial bound supplies a physical ground weight greater than \\(e^{-b_0\\alpha}\\), and hence\n\\[\nY_m(b_0)&gt;e^{-b_0\\alpha}.\n\\]\nDividing proves the first inequality.</p><p>Factor\n\\[\nP_*(x)=x(x-1/2)(x+9/25).\n\\]\nOn \\([\\ell,U]\\), it is positive and \\(xP_*'(x)&lt;4P_*(x)\\). The latter is the positivity of\n\\[\n4P_*(x)-xP_*'(x)=x(x^2-.28x-.54)\n\\]\non this interval. Thus \\(x^m/P_*(x)^2\\) is increasing for \\(m&gt;8\\). Proposition 6 therefore gives\n\\[\ne^{b_0\\alpha}\\lambda_0^mc_0\n\\le\nU^m\\frac{.003745e^{b_0\\alpha}}{P_*(U)^2}\n&lt;e^{.132}U^m.\n\\]\nThe left coefficient is below \\(1.133226\\), whereas \\(e^{.132}&gt;1.141108\\).</p><p>Combining leading and residual estimates gives\n\\[\ne^{b_0\\alpha}Y_m(b_0)\\le1+R(m).\n\\]\nSelect one physical ground eigenvector; uniqueness is not needed. Its unnormalized weight \\(g\\) exceeds \\(e^{-b_0\\alpha}\\), so the sum of all remaining weights divided by \\(g\\) is at most \\(R(m)\\). In particular, \\(R(m)\\ge0\\).</p><p>At \\(b_j=2^jb_0\\), the rest-to-ground ratio is at most \\(R(m)^{2^j}\\), because a sum of nonnegative \\(2^j\\)th powers is at most the \\(2^j\\)th power of the sum. The purity is at least the square of the normalized weight of the chosen ground eigenvector. This proves the second inequality.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Sections 5.1–5.2, including the bounds (5.5)–(5.9)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The boundary recurrence is initialized by \\( e_{142}(2)<.14,\\qquad 1-A(240,2)<.21. \\) The displayed rounded bounds satisfy Proposition 3 at each step \\(j=2,3,4\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(e_k(j)\\), \\(A(m,j)\\), \\(M_j\\), \\(k_j\\), and \\(u_j\\) from Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(F\\) from Proposition 3 and \\(R(m)\\), \\(\\epsilon(m)\\) from Lemma 7.1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let the rounded defect bounds be\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|cc|c}\n  j&amp;E_j&amp;\\eta_j&amp;\n  \\text{upper bound on \\(q\\) for the step }j\\to j+1\\\\ \\hline\n  2&amp;.14&amp;.21&amp;.142\\\\\n  3&amp;.12&amp;.155&amp;.0282\\\\\n  4&amp;.016&amp;.026&amp;.0028\\\\\n  5&amp;.001&amp;.001&amp;-\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The boundary recurrence is initialized by\n\\[\ne_{142}(2)&lt;.14,\\qquad 1-A(240,2)&lt;.21.\n\\]\nThe displayed rounded bounds satisfy Proposition 3 at each step \\(j=2,3,4\\). Consequently,\n\\[\ne_{k_5}(5)\\le.001,\\qquad\n1-A(M_5,5)\\le.001,\\qquad\nu_5&lt;.000015.\n\\]\nThe leading overlaps \\(c_0,c_1,c_2\\), and those subsequently produced by the recurrence, are positive.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 7.1 gives\n\\[\n\\epsilon(34)&lt;.0928&lt;1,\n\\]\nso \\(c_0&gt;0\\). Proposition 2 gives \\(\\rho_1\\le.953\\) and \\(\\rho_2\\le.971\\). Two applications of Lemma 3.1 yield\n\\[\ne_{78}(1)\n\\le F\\!\\left(.953^{44},.093,(1+R(78))^{-2}\\right)&lt;.133,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\ne_{142}(2)\n\\le F\\!\\left(.971^{64},.133,(1+R(142)^2)^{-2}\\right)&lt;.14.\n\\]\nAt \\(m=240\\), \\(j=2\\), Lemma 7.1 gives\n\\[\n1-A(240,2)&lt;.21.\n\\]\nThus the coupled update begins with \\(E_2=.14\\), \\(\\eta_2=.21\\). Both new overlaps have been proved positive by strict defect bounds; no overlap was assumed at the next temperature.</p><p>The lower bounds used are\n\\[\n(1+R(78))^{-2}&gt;.6180,\n\\]\n\\[\n(1+R(142)^2)^{-2}&gt;.7533,\n\\]\n\\[\n(1+R(240)^4)^{-2}&gt;.7923.\n\\]\nUsing the full expressions for \\(R(m)\\), the three upper outputs are below\n\\[\n.132129,\\qquad .137301,\\qquad .207518,\n\\]\nrespectively. The displayed lower bounds are rounded for presentation.</p><p>For the next three steps, the exponents\n\\[\n\\frac{M_j-k_j}{M_j}\n\\]\nare \\(98/240,1/2,1/2\\). The successive upper outputs for \\(E_{j+1}\\) are below\n\\[\n.117754,\\qquad .015328,\\qquad .000170,\n\\]\nand those for \\(\\eta_{j+1}\\) are below\n\\[\n.147078,\\qquad .025188,\\qquad .000432.\n\\]\nFor the latter outputs, use the displayed rounded \\(E_{j+1}\\), so each row closes with the next. This proves the table’s conclusions. Direct iteration of \\(u_{j+1}=6u_j^2\\) gives \\(u_5&lt;.000015\\).</p><p>All scalar comparisons have elementary rational verification. For \\(0\\le t\\le10\\), let\n\\[\nT_{80}(t)=\\sum_{r=0}^{80}\\frac{t^r}{r!}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nT_{80}(t)\\le e^t\\le T_{80}(t)+\\frac{2t^{81}}{81!},\n\\]\nbecause the ratio of consecutive omitted terms is at most \\(10/82&lt;1/2\\). For negative exponents, use reciprocal intervals.</p><p>Positive rational root comparisons are checked by raising both sides to integer powers. For example, the first \\(q\\)-bound is\n\\[\nd(u_3)^{49}&lt;.142^{120}.\n\\]\nAll denominators are positive. These rules and the exact rational inputs verify the finite comparisons without floating-point assumptions; the paper’s supplementary scalar certificate records them.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition 6.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For every \\(j\\ge5\\) and every integer \\(n\\) satisfying \\( M_j\\le n\\le2M_j, \\) one has \\( \\frac{Y_n^{\\mathrm{s}}(b_{j+1})} {Y_n^{\\mathrm{s}}(b_j)^2} \\ge1-4x_j. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  H_n^{\\mathrm{s}}\n  =\\sum_{r=0}^{n-2}S_r\\cdot S_{r+1}\n  -\\frac35S_0^z-\\frac35S_{n-1}^z,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  Y_n^{\\mathrm{s}}(b)\n  =\\operatorname{Tr}e^{-b(H_n^{\\mathrm{s}}+anI)},\n  \\qquad a=\\frac{700741}{500000}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(j\\ge5\\), put\n  \\[\n  b_j=2^j\\frac{49}{4},\\qquad\n  M_j=240\\,2^{j-2},\\qquad\n  x_j=10^{-1-2^{j-5}}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(j\\ge5\\) and every integer \\(n\\) satisfying\n\\[\nM_j\\le n\\le2M_j,\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n\\frac{Y_n^{\\mathrm{s}}(b_{j+1})}\n{Y_n^{\\mathrm{s}}(b_j)^2}\n\\ge1-4x_j.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(M=M_j\\). At temperature \\(b_j\\), Lemma 8.1 gives\n\\[\nQ_j(n)\\le B_M(j).\n\\]\nThis upper bound can be squared because the physical partition function, and hence \\(Q_j(n)\\), is positive.</p><p>At temperature \\(b_{j+1}\\), use the same even length \\(M=k_{j+1}\\). Proposition 7 and Lemma 8.1 give\n\\[\nQ_{j+1}(n)\n\\ge2c_{j+1}-B_M(j+1)\n\\ge(1-2x_{j+1})B_M(j+1)&gt;0.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\frac{Y_n^{\\mathrm{s}}(b_{j+1})}\n{Y_n^{\\mathrm{s}}(b_j)^2}\n\\ge\n(1-2x_{j+1})A(M,j)r_j^{n-M},\n\\]\nwhere \\(r_j=\\lambda_{j+1}/\\lambda_j^2\\).</p><p>Since \\(0&lt;r_j\\le1\\) and \\(0\\le n-M\\le M\\), Proposition 2 gives\n\\[\nr_j^{n-M}\\ge r_j^M\\ge(1-u_j)(1-u_{j+1}).\n\\]\nProposition 7 therefore yields\n\\[\n\\frac{Y_n^{\\mathrm{s}}(b_{j+1})}\n{Y_n^{\\mathrm{s}}(b_j)^2}\n\\ge\n(1-2x_{j+1})(1-x_j)(1-u_j)(1-u_{j+1}).\n\\]\nUsing\n\\[\nx_{j+1}=10x_j^2,\\qquad u_j\\le x_j,\\qquad\nu_{j+1}\\le6x_j^2,\n\\]\nthe defect of the last product is at most\n\\[\n20x_j^2+x_j+x_j+6x_j^2\n=2x_j+26x_j^2\\le4x_j,\n\\]\nbecause \\(x_j\\le.01\\). This proves the proposition.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\( |Q_j(n)-c_j|\\le B_M(j)-c_j. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(j\\ge0\\), and use the transfer operator \\(X_{b_j}\\), boundary vector \\(v_{b_j}\\), positive leading eigenvalue \\(\\lambda_j\\), and leading projection \\(P_j\\) from Propositions 1–2.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  c_j=\\|P_jv_{b_j}\\|^2,\\qquad\n  B_M(j)=\\langle v_{b_j},(|X_{b_j}|/\\lambda_j)^Mv_{b_j}\\rangle.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M\\ge2\\) be even and let \\(n\\ge M\\) be an integer.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  Q_j(n)=\\frac{Y_n^{\\mathrm{s}}(b_j)}{\\lambda_j^n}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n|Q_j(n)-c_j|\\le B_M(j)-c_j.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The leading spatial line is fixed by \\(U_P\\), and its eigenvalue is \\(+\\lambda_j\\), by Proposition 2. Its contribution to \\(Q_j(n)\\) is therefore exactly \\(c_j\\).</p><p>For every other eigenvalue \\(\\xi\\) of \\(X_{b_j}\\), let \\(P_\\xi\\) be its spectral projection. The unitary \\(U_P\\) commutes with that projection. Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\n|\\langle P_\\xi v_{b_j},U_PP_\\xi v_{b_j}\\rangle|\n\\le\\|P_\\xi v_{b_j}\\|^2.\n\\]\nThus the absolute residual is at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\xi\\ne\\lambda_j}\n(|\\xi|/\\lambda_j)^n\\|P_\\xi v_{b_j}\\|^2\n\\le B_M(j)-c_j.\n\\]\nThe series converges absolutely, being bounded by \\(\\|v_{b_j}\\|^2\\). The kernel of \\(X_{b_j}\\) contributes zero because all exponents are positive. Negative spatial eigenvalues and odd powers are included.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [2, 3, "cite"], [2, 6, "cite"], [2, 7, "cite"], [2, 9, "cite"], [2, 14, "cite"], [2, 15, "cite"], [2, 19, "cite"], [3, 7, "cite"], [3, 8, "cite"], [3, 9, "cite"], [3, 14, "cite"], [3, 15, "cite"], [3, 17, "cite"], [3, 18, "cite"], [3, 19, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [4, 7, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [6, 3, "cite"], [7, 14, "cite"], [7, 15, "cite"], [7, 17, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [8, 17, "cite"], [9, 14, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [11, 14, "cite"], [12, 11, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [13, 11, "cite"], [14, 16, "cite"], [15, 18, "cite"], [16, 17, "cite"], [17, 15, "cite"], [18, 0, "cite"], [19, 18, "cite"]]});
